Tool Command Good at GCC analyzer <code>gcc -fanalyzer -c file.c</code> (GCC 10+) leaks, double free, use after free, file-handle leaks in C, with the path that leads there clang-tidy <code>clang-tidy file.c -checks='-*,clang-analyzer-*,bugprone-*,cert-*' -- -std=c17</code> or <code>run-clang-tidy -p build</code> path-sensitive analysis plus style and API-misuse checks cppcheck <code>cppcheck --enable=warning,style --project=build/compile_commands.json</code> (<code>sudo apt install cppcheck</code>) fast, few false positives, works without a full build setup MSVC <code>/analyze</code> the Windows build of the same code ASan / UBSan rebuild with <code>-fsanitize=address,undefined</code> and run the tests real memory errors on the paths you execute Valgrind <code>valgrind --leak-check=full ./prog</code> binaries you cannot rebuild; uninitialised reads <p>Real output for this ten-line function, written in a style common in old code:</p> <pre><code>int load_cfg(const char *path, char **out)
{
    FILE *f = fopen(path, "r");
    char *buf = malloc(256);
    if (!f)
        return -1;
    if (!fgets(buf, 256, f))
        return -2;
    fclose(f);
    *out = buf;
    return 0;
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>$ gcc -std=c17 -fanalyzer -c load_cfg.c
load_cfg.c:10:16: warning: leak of 'buf' [CWE-401] [-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak]
load_cfg.c:12:16: warning: leak of FILE 'f' [CWE-775] [-Wanalyzer-file-leak]
load_cfg.c:12:16: warning: leak of 'buf' [CWE-401] [-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak]

$ clang-tidy load_cfg.c -checks='-*,clang-analyzer-*,bugprone-*,cert-*' -- -std=c17
load_cfg.c:10:17: warning: Potential leak of memory pointed to by 'buf' [clang-analyzer-unix.Malloc]
load_cfg.c:13:5: warning: the value returned by this function should not be disregarded; neglecting it may lead to errors [cert-err33-c]
</code></pre> <p>(GCC also printed a duplicate "leak of 'f'" line, omitted here; each warning comes with the full path of events in the real output.) GCC found the <code>FILE</code> leak that clang-tidy's default checks missed, and clang-tidy flagged the unchecked <code>fclose</code> that GCC did not: run more than one tool. On <code>m15_legacy_report.c</code>, clang-tidy also flags <code>atol</code> (<code>cert-err34-c</code>: "will not report conversion errors") and every <code>sprintf</code>, but it equally flags every <code>snprintf</code> as "insecure", which is noise. On an old codebase the first run produces hundreds of findings. Do not try to fix them all: record a baseline, fix by category starting with the real bug classes (leaks, overflows, use after free), and fail CI only on new findings.</p>

Standard Year What it added that you are expected to know K&amp;R C 1978 the original language of the book C89 / C90 1989 / 1990 ANSI then ISO standard: prototypes, <code>void *</code>, <code>const</code>, <code>volatile</code>, the standard library C99 1999 <code>//</code> comments; declarations anywhere and in <code>for</code>; <code>long long</code>; <code>&lt;stdint.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;inttypes.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;stdbool.h&gt;</code>; <code>inline</code>; <code>restrict</code>; variable length arrays; designated initialisers; compound literals; flexible array members; <code>snprintf</code>; <code>__func__</code>; variadic macros; <code>static</code> in array parameters; integer division defined to truncate toward zero C11 2011 <code>_Static_assert</code>; <code>_Generic</code>; <code>_Alignas</code> / <code>_Alignof</code>; <code>_Noreturn</code>; <code>_Thread_local</code>; <code>&lt;threads.h&gt;</code>; <code>&lt;stdatomic.h&gt;</code> and a memory model; anonymous structs and unions; <code>aligned_alloc</code>; <code>quick_exit</code>; <code>gets</code> removed; VLAs made optional (<code>__STDC_NO_VLA__</code>); optional Annex K (<code>_s</code> functions) C17 (also called C18) 2018 defect fixes only, no new features; <code>__STDC_VERSION__</code> is <code>201710L</code> C23 ISO/IEC 9899:2024 <code>bool</code>, <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>nullptr</code>, <code>static_assert</code>, <code>alignas</code>, <code>thread_local</code> as keywords; <code>constexpr</code> objects; <code>typeof</code>; <code>auto</code> type inference; <code>[[nodiscard]]</code>, <code>[[maybe_unused]]</code>, <code>[[fallthrough]]</code>, <code>[[deprecated]]</code>; digit separators <code>1'000'000</code>; binary literals <code>0b1010</code>; <code>= {}</code> empty initialiser; <code>#embed</code>; <code>#elifdef</code>; <code>&lt;stdckdint.h&gt;</code> (<code>ckd_add</code>, <code>ckd_mul</code>); <code>memset_explicit</code>; <code>strdup</code> standardised; <code>_BitInt(N)</code>; two's complement required; <code>void f()</code> now means "no parameters"; old K&amp;R function definitions removed <p>GCC 13 implements most of C23 under <code>-std=c2x</code>. This snippet uses several C23 features; it compiles cleanly with <code>gcc -std=c2x -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic</code> on GCC 13.3 and prints <code>1000 10 1 1 3.5 1</code>:</p> <pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
[[nodiscard]] static int f(void) { return 1; }
int main(void) {
    constexpr int N = 1'000;          /* constexpr object, digit separator */
    typeof(N) m = 0b1010;             /* typeof, binary literal */
    int *p = nullptr;                 /* nullptr */
    bool ok = true;                   /* bool is a keyword, no header */
    auto x = 3.5;                     /* type inferred: double */
    static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4);  /* message now optional */
    int arr[4] = {};                  /* empty initialiser */
    printf("%d %d %d %d %g %d\n", N, m, p == nullptr, ok, x, arr[0] + f());
    return 0;
}
</code></pre> <p>GCC 13 does not have <code>&lt;stdckdint.h&gt;</code> (it arrived in GCC 14; <code>__builtin_add_overflow</code> is the GCC 13 equivalent) or <code>#embed</code> (GCC 15). Most production C today is C99 or C11; knowing what C23 changes is a plus, assuming it in an interview answer is not.</p>

Topic C C++ <code>void *</code> conversion implicit: <code>int *p = malloc(n);</code> is correct and idiomatic needs a cast; g++ says <code>error: invalid conversion from 'void*' to 'int*' [-fpermissive]</code> Struct tags <code>struct point p;</code> unless you <code>typedef</code> it <code>point p;</code> works: the tag is a type name <code>const int N = 10;</code> a read-only object, not a constant expression: at file scope <code>int a[N];</code> is an error, at block scope it makes a VLA a constant expression: <code>int a[N];</code> is a normal array <code>const</code> at file scope external linkage internal linkage <code>bool</code> <code>_Bool</code> plus <code>&lt;stdbool.h&gt;</code> macros (keywords since C23) keyword <code>sizeof('a')</code> <code>sizeof(int)</code>: a character literal is an <code>int</code> 1: it is a <code>char</code> VLAs C99; optional since C11 not in the standard (a GCC extension) Designated initialisers any order, arrays (<code>[3] = 7</code>), nested C++20, declaration order only, no array designators Compound literals yes no (extension) <code>void f()</code> before C23: unspecified parameters; C23: no parameters no parameters <code>restrict</code> yes no (compilers offer <code>__restrict</code>) Union type punning defined: the bytes are reinterpreted undefined behaviour; use <code>memcpy</code> or <code>std::bit_cast</code> <code>int x; int x;</code> at file scope legal (tentative definitions) error: redefinition <code>goto</code> over an initialisation allowed (except into a VLA's scope) error <code>inline</code> C99 rules: an <code>inline</code> definition without one <code>extern</code> declaration in some <code>.c</code> file gives <code>undefined reference</code> at <code>-O0</code> ODR rules: just works Names in object files <code>kv_close</code> mangled: <code>_Z8kv_closeP2kv</code> <p>Name mangling and <code>extern "C"</code>. C++ encodes parameter types into symbol names so that overloads can coexist. Real <code>nm</code> output for the same function compiled as C++, with and without <code>extern "C"</code>:</p> <pre><code>0000000000000000 T _Z8kv_closeP2kv      &lt;- int kv_close(struct kv *)   (C++ linkage)
0000000000000019 T kv_close_c           &lt;- extern "C" int kv_close_c(struct kv *)
</code></pre> <p><code>_Z</code> starts a mangled name, <code>8kv_close</code> is the name with its length, <code>P2kv</code> is "pointer to the type named <code>kv</code>". A C library header used from C++ must therefore wrap its declarations, or the C++ caller looks for <code>_Z8kv_closeP2kv</code> and the link fails with <code>undefined reference</code>:</p> <pre><code>#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
int  kv_open(kv_t **out, size_t capacity);
void kv_close(kv_t *kv);
#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
</code></pre> <p>The C99 <code>inline</code> trap, real GCC 13 output for <code>inline int twice(int x) { return 2 * x; }</code> called from <code>main</code> in one file at <code>-O0</code>:</p> <pre><code>/usr/bin/ld: /tmp/ccwj7xer.o: in function `main':
inl.c:(.text+0xe): undefined reference to `twice'
</code></pre> <p>At <code>-O2</code> the call is inlined and it links, so the bug hides in release builds. In C, a plain <code>inline</code> definition is only an inline definition; one translation unit must also provide the external definition (<code>extern inline int twice(int);</code>). The everyday fix: <code>static inline</code> in headers.</p>
